# The SSH key that signs SHA256SUMS of every release (RELEASES.md).
#
# Verify a release with:
#
#   ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers -I 0xoucanxxx@gmail.com \
#       -n file -s SHA256SUMS.sig < SHA256SUMS
#
# Take this file from the git repository at the release's tag, not from the
# release page: a file published next to the signature it vouches for proves
# nothing. Compare the key with a second channel too before trusting it, such
# as the maintainer's SSH signing keys on GitHub:
# https://api.github.com/users/0xOucan/ssh_signing_keys
#
# PLACEHOLDER: no key yet. The maintainer adds ONE line, the PUBLIC key only,
# in this form (namespaces="file" limits it to signing files):
#
#   0xoucanxxx@gmail.com namespaces="file" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...
#
# Until that line exists, every verification fails, which is the intended
# behaviour: no release should be published before it is added.
